hnology and internet governance. Founded by key figures including Mitchell Baker and Brendan Eich, it emerged as a response to the growing concerns over internet accessibility, privacy, and the monopolistic tendencies of major tech corporations. As a non-profit organization, its mission transcends mere software development; it advocates for an open and accessible internet, thereby influencing global policies and practices related to digital rights and online freedoms.

